  "worked_solution": "Let's reverse the limits of integration on the known result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{3}^{0}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t & =-{\\int }_{0}^{3}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-\u27e8-6,\\,12,\\,-15\u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e86,\\,-12,\\,15\u27e9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, applying the scalar multiplication rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{3}^{0}\\frac{2}{3}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t & =\\frac{2}{3}{\\int }_{3}^{0}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot \u27e86,\\,-12,\\,15\u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e84,\\,-8,\\,10\u27e9.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
